\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Problem Formulation and GRPO}
\label{sec:problem_formulation}

We model the multi-turn agent as a POMDP where the policy $\pi_\phi(a_t \mid h_t)$ generates actions conditioned on interaction history $h_t = (o_1, a_1, \ldots, o_t)$. Episodes of $T$ steps terminate with binary reward $R \in \{0, r_+\}$. GRPO~\cite{shao2024deepseekmath} samples $K$ rollouts per task and computes group-relative advantages:
\begin{equation}
\hat{A}_k = \frac{R_k - \mu}{\sigma + \epsilon}, \quad \mu = \frac{1}{K}\sum_{j=1}^K R_j, \quad \sigma = \sqrt{\frac{1}{K}\sum_{j=1}^K (R_j - \mu)^2}
\label{eq:grpo_advantage}
\end{equation}
GRPO is episode-level by design: each rollout receives one return and one advantage, with no inherent temporal discounting.

\subsection{Step-Independent Windowing}
\label{sec:step_independent}

Agent RL frameworks face a mismatch between GRPO's episode-level design and memory constraints of long trajectories. The RAGEN framework~\cite{ragen2025} introduces \textbf{step-independent training}: the trajectory is partitioned into non-overlapping $h$-step windows, each receiving a discounted return:
\begin{equation}
R_k = \gamma^{(N - k)} \cdot R_{\text{episode}}
\label{eq:step_return}
\end{equation}
where $N = \lceil T/h \rceil$ is the number of windows. Each window is optimized as an independent GRPO sample. This reduces sequence length from $O(T)$ to $O(h)$ and multiplies the effective batch size, but fundamentally alters credit assignment.

\subsection{Dual Mechanism Analysis}
\label{sec:dual_mechanism}

We identify two mechanisms that jointly cause training failure under step-independent optimization.

\subsubsection{Mechanism 1: Policy Information Loss}
\label{sec:m1_information_loss}

Step-independent training truncates context at both inference and training time. The policy $\pi_\phi(a_t \mid h_{\text{window}_k})$ conditions on only the local $h$-step history, losing access to earlier observations. Consider a task requiring the agent to explore a drawer (step 3--4), then navigate to a countertop (step 8). With $h{=}2$, the window containing step 8 has no access to the exploration outcome. The policy cannot learn conditional strategies spanning more than $h$ steps. This imposes a \textit{constant} performance ceiling that does not change with training---no amount of optimization can recover absent information.

\subsubsection{Mechanism 2: Advantage Sign Misattribution}
\label{sec:m2_sign_misattribution}

Under step-independent training, group normalization produces advantages whose sign reflects the \textit{episode} outcome, not local action quality:
\begin{equation}
\text{sign}(\hat{A}_k) \approx \text{sign}(R_{\text{episode}} - \mu)
\label{eq:sign_correlation}
\end{equation}
In failed episodes ($R_{\text{episode}} = 0$), all windows receive negative advantages when $\mu > 0$, including windows with locally correct actions. Conversely, successful episodes reinforce all windows, including those with suboptimal actions. Unlike M1's constant ceiling, sign misattribution is \textit{cumulative}: each wrong-sign gradient step further erodes policy competence. The discount factor $\gamma^{(N-k)}$ introduces magnitude decay across positions, but sign errors are fundamentally more harmful---magnitude decay slows convergence, while sign errors cause active divergence.

\subsection{Episode-Level Training}
\label{sec:episode_level}

Episode-level training resolves both mechanisms by treating the complete trajectory as a single GRPO sample. The full history $h_t$ is available at every step (eliminating M1), and a single advantage $\hat{A}$ applies uniformly to all tokens (eliminating M2). While per-step credit remains coarse, uniform reinforcement of successful trajectories preserves good sub-strategies, whereas window-level sign errors actively destroy them. The computational cost---processing $O(T)$ tokens per sequence---is modest compared to the systematic training failure avoided. This is, in fact, GRPO's original design; step-independent windowing is the deviation.

\subsection{Factorial Experiment Design}
\label{sec:factorial_design}

To separate the two mechanisms, we implement a $2 \times 2$ factorial varying observation scope and advantage granularity: \textbf{Cell~A} (truncated $h{=}2$ + step-level advantage; both mechanisms active), \textbf{Cell~B} (truncated $h{=}2$ + episode-level advantage; only M1 active), and \textbf{Cell~D} (full trajectory + episode-level advantage; neither active). The fourth cell is degenerate (full observation with one window makes step- and episode-level advantage identical). Comparing A vs.\ B isolates sign misattribution; comparing B vs.\ D isolates information loss.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Generality beyond ALFWorld.}
The sign misattribution mechanism is not specific to RAGEN, ALFWorld, or GRPO. It arises from three structural properties that co-occur in any multi-turn agent RL setup: (a) step-level windowing, (b) sparse binary reward, and (c) group-relative normalization. When these hold, the advantage sign collapses to a proxy for episode outcome regardless of local quality (Equation~\ref{eq:sign_correlation}). Proposition~2 reveals that severity scales with task difficulty: as baseline success $p$ decreases, $P(\text{correct} \mid \text{update}) \to p$, so sign misattribution becomes \textit{more} severe precisely where credit assignment is most needed.

\paragraph{Direction correctness vs.\ credit granularity.}
Episode-level GRPO assigns uniform advantage to all tokens. At low success rates ($p{=}12.5\%$), direction correctness dominates: coarse-but-correct gradients produce slow convergence, while fine-but-wrong gradients produce divergence. As $p$ increases, the marginal value of per-step credit grows. Methods combining episode-level direction with finer credit---such as $\lambda$-annealing from $\lambda{=}1$ to $\lambda{<}1$ as the policy improves---represent a natural extension.

\paragraph{Practitioner guidance.}
For multi-turn agent RL with sparse terminal reward: (1)~if the full episode fits within context, use episode-level credit as the minimum viable approach; (2)~if the episode exceeds context capacity, use the largest feasible $h$ with episode-level advantage (our Cell~B configuration); (3)~finer-grained credit (e.g., Turn-PPO, GAGPO) should be considered only after establishing a working episode-level baseline.

\subsection{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\paragraph{Environment scope.} Our primary experiments use ALFWorld, with cross-environment validation on ScienceWorld. The theoretical argument is environment-agnostic, but additional environments would strengthen generality.

\paragraph{Scale coverage.} 8B experiments (Section~\ref{sec:cross_scale}) confirm scale invariance, but report single-seed runs due to FSDP constraints. The 1.7B factorial uses two seeds per condition.

\paragraph{Coarse episode-level credit.} For very long episodes ($T > 100$) or tasks with many independent sub-goals, uniform advantage may limit efficiency even though it preserves direction correctness.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{RL for multi-turn LLM agents.}
GRPO~\cite{shao2024deepseekmath}, originally designed for mathematical reasoning, has been adapted to multi-turn agents through step-independent windowing~\cite{ragen2025}, with subsequent work improving algorithms (GiGPO~\cite{feng2025gigpo}), reward design (Self-Guide~\cite{selfguide2026}), or training pipelines (SPEAR~\cite{spear2025}, DigiRL~\cite{digiRL2024}). None diagnoses the windowing mechanism itself as a source of systematic sign errors.

\paragraph{Credit assignment and sign misattribution evidence.}
Classical methods span the TD($\lambda$)~\cite{sutton1988td} to Monte Carlo spectrum, with GAE~\cite{schulman2016gae} interpolating via a learned value function and REINFORCE~\cite{williams1992reinforce} using episode-level returns. Several independent studies corroborate our sign misattribution diagnosis: IRC~\cite{irc2026} reports per-turn advantages frequently disagree in sign with outcome advantages (14pp degradation); MC-GRPO~\cite{mcgrpo2025} measures a 5\% sign-flip rate accounting for ${\sim}$4\% accuracy loss; GRPO-$\lambda$~\cite{grpolambda2025} proves value estimation error grows with position; and OAR~\cite{oar2026} identifies ``reward aliasing'' from coarse credit. Turn-PPO~\cite{turnppo2026}, GAGPO~\cite{gagpo2026}, MT-GRPO~\cite{mtgrpo2025}, and SWEET-RL~\cite{sweetrl2025} propose multi-turn credit assignment fixes; we complement them with a mechanistic diagnosis.

\paragraph{GRPO collapse phenomena.}
RAGEN-2~\cite{ragen2_2026} proves advantage collapse when within-prompt reward variance vanishes. GRPO-Collapse~\cite{grpocollapse2025} identifies a death spiral in Search-R1 where all tested models collapse within 300 steps. Gradient Coupling~\cite{gradientcoupling2025} argues the true failure is high input similarity across consecutive steps. These observations converge on structural instability in step-independent GRPO; our dual-mechanism diagnosis unifies them.

