\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We test three predictions: (1) step-independent training collapses while episode-level avoids collapse, (2) a factorial design separates information loss (M1) from sign misattribution (M2), and (3) window size $h$ modulates severity.

\subsection{Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

We evaluate on ALFWorld~\cite{shridhar2021alfworld}, a text-based household environment with six task types requiring sequential reasoning over up to 50 steps. Reward is binary: $R \in \{0, r_+\}$. The base policy is Qwen3-1.7B fine-tuned on 2{,}546 expert demonstrations (SFT baseline: 12.5\% in-distribution, 0\% pre-SFT). At $p{=}0.125$ with $K{=}8$, Proposition~\ref{prop:correct_sign} predicts ${\sim}80.9\%$ of gradient updates carry incorrect sign. All conditions use RAGEN~\cite{ragen2025} with GRPO~\cite{shao2024deepseekmath} ($K{=}8$, lr$=10^{-6}$, $\beta{=}0.01$, $\gamma{=}0.95$, 15 steps; full hyperparameters in Appendix~\ref{app:hyperparameters}). We report success rate on a 134-task OOD set (primary) and 32-task in-distribution set (secondary). Seed counts are noted per condition.

\subsection{Factorial Mechanism Separation}
\label{sec:factorial_experiments}

We implement the $2 \times 2$ factorial (Section~\ref{sec:factorial_design}): \textbf{Cell~A} (both mechanisms active; $h{=}2$, step-level advantage), \textbf{Cell~B} (M1 only; $h{=}2$, episode-level advantage), and \textbf{Cell~D} (neither; full trajectory, episode-level advantage). Two seeds each.

\subsection{Additional Conditions}
\label{sec:additional_conditions}

\paragraph{Dense reward.} Step-independent $h{=}2$ with $+0.15$ per valid action. Action validity rises from 60\% to 97.5\% while task success remains 0\%---the agent games format-level rewards without solving tasks.
\label{sec:results_dense}

\paragraph{REINFORCE++.} Episode-level with per-token running-mean baseline (no critic).\footnote{Originally planned as GAE; substituted after TRL~1.1.0 removed the required \texttt{AutoModelForCausalLMWithValueHead} class.}

\paragraph{Discount factor sweep.} $\gamma \in \{0.50, 0.80, 0.95, 1.00\}$ at $h{=}5$. All four collapse to 0\% within 5 steps, including $\gamma{=}1.0$ where no magnitude decay exists. This confirms sign misattribution, not discounting, is the operative mechanism.

\paragraph{Window-size sweep.} $h \in \{2, 5, 10, 20, \text{full}\}$. OOD success: $h{=}2$: 0\%, $h{=}5$: 0\%, $h{=}10$: 6.25\%, $h{=}20$: 15.6\%, episode-level: 23.88\%.% exp_id: h_sweep_series, exp029_seed1
\label{sec:results_hsweep}
A cliff between $h{=}10$ and $h{=}20$ marks where sufficient context begins to offset information loss.

\subsection{Sign Misattribution Verification}
\label{sec:sign_verification}

We directly measure the advantage signal during step-independent training ($h{=}2$) by logging per-window advantage sign, episode outcome, and local action quality. Table~\ref{tab:sign_verification} reports three statistics: sign-outcome correlation ($>0.99$, confirming advantage sign proxies episode success), credit agreement rate (52.07\%, indistinguishable from 50\% random), and point biserial correlation ($r{=}0.068$, near-zero dependence on local quality). Combined with $\gamma{=}1.0$ collapse, these results validate sign misattribution as the causal driver of training failure.

