\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

Training LLM agents for multi-turn interactive tasks requires credit assignment across long trajectories spanning 30--50 interaction steps~\cite{kim2025reflact,digiRL2024,wang2022scienceworld}. Supervised fine-tuning teaches action formatting but fails to instill compositional planning. Reinforcement learning addresses this gap, and Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO)~\cite{shao2024deepseekmath} has become preferred due to its critic-free design. Recent agent RL work~\cite{ragen2025,selfguide2026,spear2025,feng2025gigpo} shares a common, unscrutinized implementation pattern: the trajectory is decomposed into fixed-size windows, each optimized independently. We show this \textit{step-independent} default introduces a fundamental credit assignment pathology.

Step-independent training partitions trajectories into non-overlapping $h$-step windows, each receiving a discounted return from the binary episode outcome and optimized via GRPO~\cite{ragen2025,selfguide2026}. In our experiments, this produces a striking two-phase collapse at $h{=}2$: task success drops while action validity initially remains high, then continued training destroys even basic formatting. Standard remedies fail: dense reward shaping causes the agent to maximize format-level rewards (validity rises from 60\% to 97.5\%) while task success stays at 0\%. The root cause is not the RL algorithm but the windowing itself.

We trace the collapse to two mechanisms at different layers. \textbf{Policy information loss} (M1) operates at the inference layer: each window lacks access to earlier observations, imposing a constant performance ceiling. \textbf{Advantage sign misattribution} (M2) operates at the training layer: under binary reward, the advantage sign reflects episode success rather than local action quality. At $p{=}12.5\%$ with $K{=}8$, approximately 80.9\% of gradient updates for locally correct windows point in the wrong direction. Sign errors cause active divergence, not merely slow convergence. Episode-level GRPO eliminates both mechanisms by treating the full trajectory as a single sample, achieving 28.1\% validation success where step-independent collapses to 0\%. Cross-scale verification at 8B confirms both failure and fix are scale-invariant (90.3\% OOD vs.\ 1.5\% collapse).

Our contributions: (1)~\textbf{Dual mechanism diagnosis}: we formalize information loss and sign misattribution as distinct failure modes with different temporal behaviors (constant ceiling vs.\ cumulative degradation). (2)~\textbf{Empirical validation}: factorial mechanism separation, window-size ablation, direct sign measurement, and cross-scale verification at 1.7B and 8B. (3)~\textbf{Episode-level fix}: we show GRPO's original episode-level design simultaneously resolves both mechanisms, and analyze why the community defaulted to the broken step-independent paradigm.

